//! The page's prose, for a reader with new eyes: one who knows neither
//! geometry nor machine learning nor X_eTaL. `[shown|id]` marks a word
//! that opens its plain explanation (docs/glossary.md, the pages'
//! glossary); `**words**` is bold. Kept apart from the page so a test
//! can check every mark names an idea the glossary has and no sentence
//! passes a verdict. No result lives here: the numbers are the run's.

pub const LEDE: &str = "A deep network can carry several streams of numbers side by side and mix them at every layer. What may the mixing be allowed to do? This page takes the matrices that do the mixing one at a time: the ones that only average (doubly stochastic), the ones that may do anything (free), the ones that only rotate (orthogonal), and what each does to a signal mixed again and again. Nothing is trained here; the next part stacks them in a network.";

pub const HINT: &str = "New to this? A word with a dotted underline opens a plain explanation when you click it (or press Enter on it); click again to close it. The buttons pick a point on the path from the identity to uniform mixing; picking runs nothing, every number was computed by the program at the bottom when the page opened. A colored square is a matrix entry: hover or tap it, or move through the squares with the arrow keys once one has the focus, to read its value; the numbers are also written in the squares.";

pub const QUESTION: [&str; 3] = [
    "When a network carries several streams of numbers side by side and mixes them at every layer, what may the mixing be allowed to do?",
    "A deep network passes a [residual stream|residual-stream] from layer to layer, each layer adding its small change. [Hyper-connections|hyper-connections] widen that one stream into several and let a learned matrix mix them at every layer. Free, those matrices multiplied through many layers can grow or shrink the signal by large factors; [mHC|mhc] holds each to the [doubly stochastic|doubly-stochastic] matrices, which can only average. This page shows, on matrices of size 3, what that constraint allows and forbids.",
    "It shows four things: how a layer over several streams is written; how many numbers a doubly stochastic matrix of size 3 has free, and a path through them; how [Sinkhorn's iteration|sinkhorn] turns any positive matrix into one, worked in full; and what a doubly stochastic, a free and an orthogonal matrix each do to a signal mixed one, ten and a hundred times.",
];

pub const BACKGROUND: [&str; 5] = [
    "A **matrix** here is a square table of numbers, 3 rows by 3 columns. Multiplying a list of three numbers by it gives three new numbers, each a weighted sum of the old ones: row i of the matrix holds the weights that make new number i. A **stream** is a list of numbers a network passes along; with three streams, the numbers at one position form a list of three that the matrix mixes.",
    "A residual block keeps a stream x and adds what a layer F (a function computed by the network's block) produces from it: x becomes x + F(x). Hyper-connections (Zhu et al., 2025) keep n streams, a table of n rows, and at every layer read one input from them with weights a, mix them by an n by n matrix H, and write the layer's output back with weights b. The mHC paper (Xie et al., 2025, its equation 3) writes the layer as X becomes H X + b F(a' X), with a and b learned and H constrained (a' X, with a written as a row, is the weighted sum of the rows of X); here a takes the mean of the streams and b writes to each with weight 1, a choice inside the range mHC allows b, 0 to 2.",
    "A matrix is [doubly stochastic|doubly-stochastic] when no entry is negative and every row and every column sums to 1. Its rows summing to 1 make each new stream a weighted average of the old ones; its columns summing to 1 keep the streams' mean. The identity (each stream kept) and uniform mixing (every stream replaced by the mean) are both doubly stochastic; so is a **permutation matrix**, a 1 in each row and column and 0 elsewhere, which only reorders the streams.",
    "A matrix's [singular values|singular-value] say how much it can stretch a list of numbers, largest first; its [gain|mixing-gain], as mHC measures it, is the largest sum of the sizes of a row's entries (forward: what one output can draw from all the inputs) and of a column's (backward: the same for the training signal, the gradient, on its way back through the layer). An **orthogonal** matrix keeps every length (its singular values are all 1) and may have negative entries; the set of them is the [orthogonal group|orthogonal-group].",
    "The set of doubly stochastic matrices of one size is a [polytope|polytope] (the Birkhoff polytope): flat faces, straight edges and corners, the corners being the permutation matrices. mHC's name says \"manifold-constrained\"; a [manifold|manifold] is flat up close everywhere, which a polytope is not at its corners and edges, so this repository calls the set a polytope and the mixing constrained to it, a [constraint set|constraint-set]. A polytope's faces are flat, but where faces meet (an edge, a corner) they meet at an angle, and there the set is not flat up close: that, not its having a boundary, is what keeps it from being a smooth manifold.",
];

pub const RATIONALE: [&str; 2] = [
    "Before stacking matrices in a network (the next part), look at one at a time: count its freedom, walk a path through the set, make one from a free matrix and watch the errors fall, and mix one signal with each kind again and again. Mixing a hundred times is what a stack a hundred layers deep does to the part of the signal the layers leave alone, so the powers of a single matrix are a first look at depth.",
    "What the constraint should do, by the definitions: a doubly stochastic matrix keeps the streams' mean exactly and cannot enlarge their differences (its largest singular value is 1), so repeated it shrinks the streams toward their mean; a free matrix's powers may grow or die away; an orthogonal one keeps the length and may let the streams' differences live on. The page measures each.",
];

pub const PURPOSE: [&str; 2] = [
    "This page is the matrices alone: one matrix of each kind, chosen or made by the program, not learned and not depending on the input. Actual mHC learns matrices that depend on the input at every layer; this page's single control (a point on one path) does not. Every number, matrix and curve comes from the X_eTaL program shown at the bottom, run in your browser when the page opened.",
    "It compares three kinds of mixing on the same signal, doubly stochastic, free and orthogonal, with four matrices (the free one also scaled down). The figures are marked [measured or analytical|measured-analytical]: the matrices, sums, errors, singular values and signals are measured (computed by the program); the counting of free entries is analytical, from the definition, and checked by the program against Matrix's rank of the conditions.",
];

pub const INTUITION: [&str; 3] = [
    "Three cups of water on a table, and a rule for pouring between them at every step. A doubly stochastic rule takes from each cup and gives to each cup so that every cup ends with as much as one cup's share of what came in, and no cup's water is counted twice: the total stays the same and the cups' levels can only even out. Pour a hundred times and the levels are all the same: the mean was kept, the differences poured away.",
    "A free rule may fill cups from nothing or let water vanish: pour a hundred times and the cups overflow or run dry. An orthogonal rule is not pouring at all but a reshuffling with signs, which can swap and turn the levels round without evening them out and without changing the total size: the differences live on, at the price of rules that subtract.",
    "Deep networks meet these as a choice about the mixing part of each layer: averaging cannot enlarge the streams and, repeated, forgets their differences; free mixing may blow up or die away, and a free positive matrix repeated also loses the original differences, turning every signal toward one direction while scaling it; a reshuffling with signs keeps the size. What the layers' own added outputs do through depth is the next part's question.",
];

pub const MATHEMATICS: [&str; 6] = [
    "One stream: x becomes x + F(x), here F(x) = tanh(W x) with W a seeded 4 by 4 matrix (seeded: drawn at random from a fixed seed, so every run gets the same numbers; tanh squeezes each number into -1 to 1; a stand-in for a network's block). n streams, X with n rows: X becomes H X + b F(a' X), where a' X is the weighted sum of the rows with weights a (here each 1/n: the mean), F of it is one row, and b F writes that row to each stream with weight b (here each 1). When every stream starts as the same x, the weights a sum to 1 and b = 1, any H whose rows sum to 1 gives H X = X and the layer gives n copies of x + F(x): in that sense the many-stream layer contains the one-stream block (doubly stochastic is more than this needs).",
    "A doubly stochastic n by n matrix has n^2 entries and 2 n sums fixed at 1. The sums are not all independent: the rows' sums add to n and so do the columns', so any 2 n - 1 of the conditions give the last. That leaves n^2 - (2 n - 1) = (n - 1)^2 entries free: for n = 3, nine entries, five independent sums, four free. The program checks the five by Matrix's rank of the six conditions written as rows (one per sum, a 1 for each entry the sum includes), not by the formula. Choose the top-left 2 by 2 block and the rest is fixed by the sums (if it comes out not negative).",
    "The path from the identity to uniform mixing is H(t) = (1 - t) I + t J / n, I the identity, J the matrix of ones, t from 0 to 1: a mix of two doubly stochastic matrices, so doubly stochastic (the set is convex: a straight line between two of its points stays in it). Its singular values are 1 (along the direction of all-equal streams, which it keeps) and 1 - t (along every difference, which it shrinks by 1 - t).",
    "Sinkhorn's iteration from a positive matrix A: divide each row by its sum, then each column by its sum; repeat. After each sweep the columns sum to 1 exactly and the rows to within an error that tends to 0 (Sinkhorn 1964); here it fell at every sweep (measured). mHC makes A by taking the exponential of each of a table of learned numbers (so every entry is positive) and stops after 20 sweeps. Here the row and column errors are the largest differences of a sum from 1.",
    "A signal of n streams of one number each, v, mixed m times by H is H^m v. Its mean is (1' H^m v) / n (1' the row of ones, so 1' w is the sum of w), kept for every m when the columns of H sum to 1; its spread is the size of v less its mean (a size, or length, is the square root of the sum of the squares), which each mix multiplies by a factor no larger than H's second singular value. A free matrix's powers grow or die like the size of its largest eigenvalue to the m-th power; its largest singular value is an upper bound on that rate, and the page measures the rate. An orthogonal Q keeps the length of v for every m (and may change its mean and spread). The orthogonal matrix used is the one nearest the free A in the sum of squared entry differences, its polar factor U' V from A's singular value decomposition A = U' diag(s) V (Matrix's s_vd; U' is U turned so its rows become columns).",
    "The program's words: hc:l_ayer, hc:b_lend, hc:m_ean, hc:s_pread, hc:s_umErrors, hc:s_tochasticOff, hc:o_rthogonalOff, hc:g_ain (Mixing, this repository's library); u:s_weep (Sinkhorn's sweep, written out: matrix scaling is asked of X_eTaL-libraries, ask GL3, as is the polar factor); mx:s_ingular and mx:s_vd (Matrix's).",
];

pub const EXPERIMENT: [&str; 2] = [
    "What is measured: one stream's block and three copies of it through the many-stream layer; the count of free entries; eleven points on the path from the identity to uniform mixing, each with its row and column sums, smallest entry, singular values and distance from the set; Sinkhorn's iteration for 20 sweeps from a seeded positive matrix, one sweep worked, the errors by sweep and the sweep at which both are under 1e-6; four matrices (Sinkhorn's result, the free matrix it came from, that matrix divided by twice its largest singular value, and the orthogonal matrix nearest it) each mixing one signal 1, 10 and 100 times, the rate per mix its length changed at, with the mean, spread and length at each, and each matrix's singular values, gain and distances from the two sets; the six permutation matrices; and a step of 0.1 either way from a corner and from the middle of the set.",
    "Written before the run: what would count against the constraint doing what its definition says is a doubly stochastic matrix that changes the mean, enlarges the spread, or has a singular value above 1; or Sinkhorn's errors not falling. What would count against \"free mixing can blow up or die away\" is a free matrix whose powers stay bounded and away from 0 here; against \"orthogonal keeps length\", a change of length; against \"orthogonal keeps the differences\", a spread after 100 mixes well below the start. The free matrices are chosen so that the outcomes follow from their singular values before any mixing (all entries positive; then divided by twice the largest singular value): they illustrate the definitions rather than test them. Each matrix is one seeded example, not learned and not depending on the input, mixing one signal; a free matrix scaled so that its largest eigenvalue's size is exactly 1 would neither blow up nor die, and is not shown.",
];

